    <title>Date Calculations</title>

    <p>Open <gui>Date Calculation</gui> from the primary menu to compare dates
    or move forward or backward by a number of days. Enter dates as
    <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>, for example <code>2000-02-29</code>.</p>

    <section id="difference">
        <title>Compare two dates</title>
        <steps>
            <item><p>Choose <gui>Between two dates</gui>.</p></item>
            <item><p>Enter the start and end dates.</p></item>
            <item><p>Select <gui>Calculate</gui> or press <key>Enter</key>.</p></item>
        </steps>
        <p>The elapsed number of days is the end date minus the start date.
        It is negative when the end date comes first. Leap days count
        February 29 occurrences from the earlier date up to, but excluding,
        the later date. Equal dates give zero for both results.</p>
        <example><p>From <code>2000-02-28</code> to <code>2000-03-01</code>
        there are 2 elapsed days and 1 leap day. Ending on
        <code>2000-02-29</code> instead gives 1 elapsed day and 0 leap days.</p></example>
    </section>

    <section id="offset">
        <title>Add or subtract days</title>
        <steps>
            <item><p>Choose <gui>Add days</gui> or <gui>Subtract days</gui>.</p></item>
            <item><p>Enter the start date and a whole number of days.</p></item>
            <item><p>Select <gui>Calculate</gui> or press <key>Enter</key>.</p></item>
        </steps>
        <p>The result includes leap days and month and year boundaries.
        A negative number reverses the operation: adding -1 day is the same
        as subtracting 1 day.</p>
        <example><p>Adding 1 day to <code>2000-02-28</code> gives
        <code>2000-02-29</code>. Subtracting 1 day from
        <code>2000-03-01</code> gives the same result.</p></example>
    </section>

    <note><p>Dates use the Gregorian calendar and must be between
    <code>0001-01-01</code> and <code>9999-12-31</code>. Invalid dates,
    non-integer day counts and results outside that range display an error.
    Times of day and daylight-saving changes do not affect these calculations.</p></note>
</page>
